let __v_157 = Value::from("Paracetamol or ibuprofen is what the counter has for a headache: take the dose on the pack with water and something to eat, and one painkiller at a time to start. Taken more than a couple of days a week for months, either can cause headaches of its own. A headache that came on in seconds, is the worst you have ever had, or comes with a fever and a stiff neck, weakness, confusion or a change in vision is a 999 call, not a tablet.");
frame.store("_HEADACHE_CARD", __v_157.clone())?;
let __v_158 = Value::from("Ibuprofen is what a pharmacist will suggest for a strained back, with paracetamol alongside if you need it, and a heat pad; keep moving, because rest makes it stiffer. Say yes to their questions about your stomach, kidneys, heart and blood thinners, as ibuprofen is not for everyone. Numbness between your legs, a weak leg, or trouble passing water is a same-day GP appointment.");
frame.store("_BACK_CARD", __v_158.clone())?;
let __v_159 = Value::from("Ibuprofen works better than paracetamol for period pain, and starting it the day before helps; the pharmacist can add codeine (co-codamol) or suggest mefenamic acid if that is not enough. A heat pad and moving about ease it too. Pain that stops you living normally, or bleeding that soaks a pad an hour, is worth a GP appointment.");
frame.store("_PERIOD_CARD", __v_159.clone())?;
let __v_160 = Value::from("Paracetamol or ibuprofen, plus a medicated lozenge, is what the counter has for a sore throat, and most settle within a week without anything else. Ask about a spray with a local anaesthetic if swallowing is the worst part. See a GP if you cannot swallow fluids, cannot open your mouth wide, or it lasts more than two weeks.");
frame.store("_THROAT_CARD", __v_160.clone())?;
let __v_161 = Value::from("For a cough the pharmacist has honey-based syrups and simple linctus; the evidence for cough medicines is weak, and honey in warm water does about as well, with paracetamol or ibuprofen for the aches. Ask about a cough medicine for a night cough only - a chesty one is better left to clear. A cough with breathlessness, blood, chest pain, a fever that will not go, or more than three weeks needs a doctor.");
frame.store("_COUGH_CARD", __v_161.clone())?;
let __v_162 = Value::from("Nothing cures a cold, so the counter is for the symptoms: paracetamol or ibuprofen for aches and fever, a decongestant spray or tablet for a blocked nose, and honey for the cough. Decongestant tablets are not for you if you have high blood pressure, heart trouble or take an MAOI, so say so at the counter. Most clear in a week to ten days; a cold getting worse after a week, or a fever with breathlessness, needs a GP.");
frame.store("_COLD_CARD", __v_162.clone())?;
let __v_163 = Value::from("A once-a-day antihistamine - loratadine, cetirizine or the pharmacy's own brand, which is the same tablet for less - is the first thing to try for hay fever, and a steroid nose spray or eye drops can be added if the nose and eyes are where it is. The spray works best started a week or two before your season. Wheezing or a tight chest with it is a GP question.");
frame.store("_HAY_FEVER_CARD", __v_163.clone())?;
let __v_164 = Value::from("An antacid or an alginate (Gaviscon and the like) settles heartburn in minutes, and a 14-day course of a tablet such as omeprazole is the next step if it keeps coming back - the pharmacist can suggest both. Losing a little weight, smaller meals and nothing to eat in the three hours before bed do the rest. Trouble swallowing, weight loss or pain on swallowing is a GP appointment rather than a repeat bottle.");
frame.store("_HEARTBURN_CARD", __v_164.clone())?;
let __v_165 = Value::from("The counter has oral rehydration sachets (Dioralyte) and, if you have to travel, loperamide; the fluid is the part that matters, and most of it settles in two to three days. Keep eating plain food and stay off dairy and alcohol until it does. Blood in it, a high fever, or not keeping any water down needs a GP - and 111 if you are frail, pregnant or very young.");
frame.store("_DIARRHOEA_CARD", __v_165.clone())?;
let __v_166 = Value::from("Start with a bulk-forming laxative such as ispaghula (Fybogel) or a stool softener, with more water and fibre, and give it a few days - the pharmacist can add a short course of a stimulant laxative if nothing has moved. Ask before using them regularly, as the bowels get used to it. Constipation that comes with bleeding, weight loss, or a change that lasts weeks is worth a GP look.");
frame.store("_CONSTIPATION_CARD", __v_166.clone())?;
let __v_167 = Value::from("Thrush is an itch and a thick white discharge, and a pharmacist can treat it without a prescription: a clotrimazole pessary or cream, or a single fluconazole tablet if you are not pregnant and not on certain medicines - say what else you take. The itch eases within a few days, and the whole course still needs finishing. See a GP if it is the first time you have had it, it keeps returning, or there is bleeding, pain or a smelly discharge. Cotton rather than lace, nothing perfumed down there, and no douching, is what stops it coming back.");
frame.store("_THRUSH_CARD", __v_167.clone())?;
let __v_168 = Value::from("For the common spinning kind, the fix is the Epley manoeuvre rather than a tablet - a GP or physio can do it in one visit and you can learn it - while the counter can give prochlorperazine (Stemetil) for a few days to soften the spinning. It makes you drowsy, so no driving on it. Vertigo with double vision, slurred speech, weakness down one side, or a sudden bad headache is a 999 call.");
frame.store("_VERTIGO_CARD", __v_168.clone())?;
let __v_169 = Value::from("The counter has a short course of a sedating antihistamine (diphenhydramine, promethazine) for a few nights, and melatonin on prescription or from a pharmacist in some places; all of them leave you groggy and none is a long-term answer. The routine does more: the same wake-up time, no screens or caffeine late, and a cool dark room. Ask before mixing any of it with alcohol, antidepressants or other sedatives; weeks of broken sleep is a GP conversation.");
frame.store("_SLEEP_CARD", __v_169.clone())?;
let __v_170 = Value::from("A pharmacist can treat a simple bladder infection in women aged 16 to 64 with a short course of an antibiotic (trimethoprim or nitrofurantoin) - ask at the counter, as many pharmacies run the scheme. Drink plenty and take paracetamol for the burn. Blood in the urine, a fever, pain in your back or side, being pregnant, or being a man with these symptoms means a GP the same day.");
frame.store("_CYSTITIS_CARD", __v_170.clone())?;
let __v_171 = Value::from("Paracetamol or ibuprofen is the counter's answer for earache, and most in adults settle in a few days as the cold behind it clears; ask about drops only if the ear itself is itchy or weeping, as drops do not help a middle-ear infection. Nothing down the ear canal, no cotton buds. Discharge, a high fever, or a child under two with earache is a GP appointment.");
