let __v_361 = Value::from("Paracetamol is the painkiller to take in pregnancy - the lowest dose that works, for the shortest time - and ibuprofen and aspirin are the ones to leave alone unless a doctor prescribes them, especially after 20 weeks. Ask the pharmacist to check any other box against the pregnancy, including cold and flu remedies, because several carry a second ingredient that is not advised; a headache that will not shift, or one that comes with swelling, blurred vision or pain under the ribs, is a same-day call to the midwife.");
frame.store("_PREGNANT_CARD", __v_361.clone())?;
let __v_362 = Value::from("For a child the pharmacist will weigh the dose by age, so ask for children's paracetamol or ibuprofen and give it by the spoon or syringe in the box, never a kitchen spoon. Honey helps a cough over the age of one, and cough and cold medicines are not licensed for children under six, so skip those. A baby under three months with a fever, or a child who is limp, breathing fast or not drinking, is a same-day doctor rather than the counter.");
frame.store("_CHILD_CARD", __v_362.clone())?;
